# A merge-group CI job's wall-time budget is 20 minutes (helpers/job-budget.py, milestone 721 (each merge-group CI job has a 20-minute budget)).
# A job that must exceed it gets a line here, in the same diff that lets it grow:
#
#     <job> <minutes> <reason, in words>
#
# `check` reads the minutes as that job's budget, warns at 85% of it (§254 (a gate prints what each item cost)) and fails above it.
# `timeout-minutes` in ci.yml may then be at most the new budget plus five. A line with no reason is
# refused, and so is one that does not raise the budget above 20. On 2026-10-03 the slowest job,
# `cpu-matrix`, ran a 15.0 minute median and a 16.6 minute maximum. It reached 19.8 on
# 2026-10-06 and failed at 20.1 to 21.0 on slow runners, because each model's boot of the growing
# riscv64 suite went from 1.6 to 3.1 minutes; it was split into two `cpu-matrix-shard` jobs rather
# than given a line here (ci.yml, above that job, has the measurement).
# `test` reached 17.4 to 18.4 on 2026-10-12 and was split the same way, into two `test-shard`
# jobs by architecture (#1930; ci.yml, above that job, has the measurement).
falsify-shard 25 a shard planned at 17 minutes of replay must outlive one record that runs to its own 15-minute limit, so it can report it (verify.yml, above that job)
fuzz 22 eighteen targets at 60 seconds each are 18.5 minutes of fuzzing and about a minute of build and install, which measured 20.1 minutes on #2991 with no crash found; a nineteenth target means splitting the job into shards (ci.yml, above the fuzz job)
